examples.question_text:
How many axes of symmetry does this non-equilateral isosceles triangle have?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0,2)--(-1.2,-1)--(1.2,-1)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Fold along the line through the top vertex and the midpoint of the base. The two halves match exactly. No other fold makes them match, so there is $1$ axis of symmetry.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0,2)--(-1.2,-1)--(1.2,-1)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,-1.35)--(0,2.35);
\path (0.15,1.5) node[right] {axis of symmetry};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
What is the order of rotational symmetry of this regular hexagon?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0:1.4)--(60:1.4)--(120:1.4)--(180:1.4)--(240:1.4)--(300:1.4)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Rotate the hexagon about its centre. It matches its starting position at $60^{\circ}$, $120^{\circ}$, $180^{\circ}$, $240^{\circ}$, $300^{\circ}$ and $360^{\circ}$.

There are $6$ matching positions in one full turn, so the order of rotational symmetry is $6$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (0:1.4)--(60:1.4)--(120:1.4)--(180:1.4)--(240:1.4)--(300:1.4)--cycle;
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[dotted] (0,0)--(0:1.4);
\draw[dotted] (0,0)--(60:1.4);
\draw[->] (0:0.65) arc[start angle=0,end angle=60,radius=0.65];
\path (30:0.95) node {$60^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
State the number of lines of symmetry and the order of rotational symmetry of this parallelogram. It is neither a rectangle nor a rhombus.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (-1.8,-0.8)--(1,-0.8)--(1.8,0.8)--(-1,0.8)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
No fold line makes the halves match, so there are $0$ lines of symmetry.

A $180^{\circ}$ turn about the centre swaps opposite vertices and leaves the shape unchanged. It matches at $180^{\circ}$ and $360^{\circ}$, so its rotational symmetry has order $2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw[thick] (-1.8,-0.8)--(1,-0.8)--(1.8,0.8)--(-1,0.8)--cycle;
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\path (-1.8,-0.8) node[below left] {$A$};
\path (1,-0.8) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (1.8,0.8) node[above right] {$C$};
\path (-1,0.8) node[above left] {$D$};
\path (0,0) node[above] {centre};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The half-turn swaps $A$ with $C$ and $B$ with $D$.

